==================================================================
DAY 49 - PART A: THEORY
Cost, Cleanup, and Running EKS in Production
Time: 45 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHERE THE MONEY GOES
-----------------------
  Control plane fee         fixed per cluster per hour
  Nodes (EC2)               usually the biggest part
  Load balancers            per LB per hour + traffic (LCUs)
  NAT gateways              per hour + per GB processed (surprisingly
                            expensive with lots of image pulls/egress)
  EBS volumes / snapshots   per GB-month, even when detached
  Data transfer             cross-AZ traffic, internet egress
  Logs/metrics              CloudWatch ingestion and storage
  Extended-support versions much higher control plane fee


2. COST CONTROLS
----------------
- Right-size requests (Week 5) -> fewer nodes
- Autoscaling + Spot + Karpenter consolidation
- One shared ingress/ALB instead of many LoadBalancer Services
- VPC endpoints for ECR/S3 to cut NAT traffic
- Delete unused PVs, snapshots, LBs, old ECR images (lifecycle rules)
- Tag everything; use Cost Explorer; tools like Kubecost/OpenCost
- Non-prod clusters: scale to zero at night, or create on demand


3. PRODUCTION CHECKLIST FOR A MANAGED CLUSTER
---------------------------------------------
Cluster
  [ ] infrastructure as code (Terraform/OpenTofu, eksctl config in Git)
  [ ] supported Kubernetes version + upgrade plan every ~4-6 months
  [ ] private API endpoint or restricted public CIDRs
  [ ] secrets encryption with KMS
  [ ] control plane logs (audit) enabled
  [ ] nodes in private subnets, 3 AZs, IMDSv2 hop limit 1
Access
  [ ] SSO-based human access, least privilege, no shared admin
  [ ] workload identity for every app needing cloud APIs
Workloads
  [ ] requests/limits, probes, PDBs, topology spread across zones
  [ ] Pod Security restricted + policy engine (Kyverno/Gatekeeper)
  [ ] NetworkPolicies (VPC CNI network policy or Cilium/Calico)
  [ ] images from a private registry, scanned, pinned by tag/digest
Operations
  [ ] GitOps deployments (Week 8)
  [ ] metrics, logs, alerts (Week 8)
  [ ] backups: Velero for cluster objects, DB backups, tested restores
  [ ] cost monitoring and budgets


4. MULTI-CLUSTER / ENVIRONMENT STRATEGY
---------------------------------------
Common: separate clusters per environment (dev/stage/prod) and often
per region; namespaces per team inside. Blue/green CLUSTER upgrades
are an alternative to in-place upgrades.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Nodes, NAT, LBs, storage, and data transfer drive cost.
- Delete in the right order; verify with the billing page.
- A production cluster is code + security + observability + backups.
